# Common loon

The common loon breeds on clear, deep lakes across Canada, the northern United
States, Greenland and Iceland. It takes perch, sucker and crayfish by descending
from the surface and pursuing them below it, propelled by large webbed feet set
at the very rear of the body. Bones heavier than those of most flying relatives, and
the ability to compress air out of the plumage, let it sink almost without a
ripple when it chooses to. Its yodel and tremolo carry for kilometres over still
water and are, for many people, the sound of the northern summer. See
[[gaviidae]].
